1
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:10,167
Pulse anatomy. The lifecycle of one request, from bytes on disk, to the JSON envelope.

2
00:00:10,967 --> 00:00:18,911
Every cohort is one self-describing file. It opens with a nine-byte header: eight magic bytes, then a format version.

3
00:00:19,711 --> 00:00:27,314
Next comes the schema block. It names every field with its type, whether it can be null, its byte offset and its bit position.

4
00:00:28,114 --> 00:00:35,546
Only a newer, format-two file adds an extension block, for parent groups and vector fields. This one is format one.

5
00:00:36,346 --> 00:00:43,053
Dictionaries for categorical and set fields sit inline, so every stored code can be read back as its label.

6
00:00:43,853 --> 00:00:53,482
Then come the records, each one exactly seventeen bytes wide. So Pulse counts records with simple arithmetic: payload length, divided by stride.

7
00:00:54,282 --> 00:01:03,826
A request is admitted gate by gate. Decoding is strict, then the profile gate and item fan-out run. All three need nothing but the request itself.

8
00:01:04,626 --> 00:01:13,231
Opening the cohort reads only the header and the schema. With the schema in hand, smart defaults infer the grouper type the request left out.

9
00:01:14,031 --> 00:01:22,423
Field references, weights and resource limits are all checked against the schema alone. Watch the counter: not one record has been read.

10
00:01:23,223 --> 00:01:32,468
A misspelt key is never ignored. This request says filters, not filterers, so the very first gate refuses it, and suggests the right key.

11
00:01:33,268 --> 00:01:40,871
Request B asks for more memory than this instance allows. The limits preflight refuses it, with zero records decoded.

12
00:01:41,671 --> 00:01:50,148
This cohort turns out to be a shard archive: four pulse shards, under one canonical schema. Now the engine chooses how to run.

13
00:01:50,948 --> 00:01:56,823
Every operator in request A can merge its state, so the work fans out, with one worker per shard.

14
00:01:57,623 --> 00:02:06,228
Inside each worker, records stream through the loop one at a time. The decoder reads only the three columns the request actually names.

15
00:02:07,028 --> 00:02:16,316
A range filter drops anyone outside eighteen to sixty-five, and counts what passed, and what didn't. Those counts become components of the response.

16
00:02:17,116 --> 00:02:26,809
A formula attribute adds income in thousands. The category grouper routes each record by region, and the average aggregator updates its running state.

17
00:02:27,609 --> 00:02:34,038
The streaming arm keeps no rows at all, just state: four buckets, each holding a count and a running sum.

18
00:02:34,838 --> 00:02:39,475
Each shard runs that same loop over its own records, with fresh operator state.

19
00:02:40,275 --> 00:02:45,830
The workers finish whenever they finish. Here, shard three is first, then one, four, and two.

20
00:02:46,630 --> 00:02:54,467
Partials merge in shard order, never finish order. Shard one merged as soon as it was done. The rest waited for shard two.

21
00:02:55,267 --> 00:03:01,312
Sums are bit-identical on any number of workers, so the answer matches a serial run byte for byte.

22
00:03:02,112 --> 00:03:07,389
In a crosstab, each record lands in the cell where its row key and its column key meet.

23
00:03:08,189 --> 00:03:18,522
Record two has a region, but no plan, so it never reaches a cell. And so it reaches no margin either. East stays at eighteen, and the grand total at sixty-eight.

24
00:03:19,322 --> 00:03:22,551
A record counts in a margin only if it reached a cell.

25
00:03:23,351 --> 00:03:29,418
So a column percent is a share of those asked. Half of the sixteen on the pro plan are in the East.

26
00:03:30,218 --> 00:03:39,506
Now add tests, a regression and an overlay to request A. Result rows come first, then components, which count what each figure stands on.

27
00:03:40,306 --> 00:03:47,866
This regression's two predictors are collinear, so its slot fails alone. It carries its own error, and everything else survives.

28
00:03:48,666 --> 00:03:57,250
A slot-failed warning is lifted into the envelope, beside every other runtime warning. Under strict mode, the run would fail instead.

29
00:03:58,050 --> 00:04:07,573
Tests report a raw p-value. The multiplicity fold adds an adjusted one beside it. East passes raw, but not once four tests are corrected.

30
00:04:08,373 --> 00:04:18,045
An overlay attaches as a read-only sibling: each region's average over the sum of all four, times 100. The data itself is untouched.

31
00:04:18,845 --> 00:04:25,786
Return shaping removes whatever the caller excluded. An excluded part is absent from the output, not null.

32
00:04:26,586 --> 00:04:34,359
Finally, the envelope wraps it all: format version one point one, the data, and errors and warnings that are always lists.

33
00:04:35,159 --> 00:04:42,868
That is the life of a Pulse request. Decode, admit, open, check, choose an arm, loop, merge, fold, shape, and wrap.
